## Why This Is Asked

Tests the ability to architect heterogeneous retrieval with dynamic chunking, versioned embeddings, and streaming results. It also probes freshness, updates, and cross-doc weighting in a unified vector store.

## Key Concepts

- Heterogeneous doc types
- Per-type dynamic chunking
- Versioned embeddings and metadata
- Cross-document re-ranking
- Streaming partial results

## Code Example

```javascript
// Skeleton: per-type chunk sizes and basic chunking hook
const CHUNK_SPECS = { api: 512, policy: 1024, incident: 1024 };
function chunkDocument(doc){
  const max = CHUNK_SPECS[doc.type] ?? 512;
  // simple approach: split into sentences then group by max tokens
  const sentences = splitIntoSentences(doc.text);
  const chunks = [];
  let cur = '';
  for (const s of sentences){
    if ((cur + s).length > max){ chunks.push(cur.trim()); cur = s; }
    else { cur += s; }
  }
  if (cur) chunks.push(cur.trim());
  return chunks;
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you handle updates to docs and avoid stale embeddings?
- What metrics and tests validate per-type chunking efficacy and latency?

## Why This Is Asked

Tests a practical, end-to-end RAG pipeline with real-world constraints: chunking, embedding stability, vector DB tenancy, latency targets, and robust failover. It also probes how to evaluate recall/latency at scale across multiple tenants.

## Key Concepts

- Chunking strategy: 512-token blocks aligned to maintain context.
- Embeddings: stable models (SBERT or OpenAI Ada) with versioned updates.
- Vector DB tenancy: per-repo namespaces and strict access controls.
- Freshness: index aging, re-embedding on document updates.
- Latency: retrieval latency targets (p95/p50) and caching.
- Failover: lexical BM25 fallback and provenance surface.

## Code Example

```javascript
// Pseudo: split text into 512-token chunks using a simple tokenizer (word-based placeholder)
function chunkDoc(text) {
  const tokens = text.split(/\s+/);
  const chunkSize = 512;
  const chunks = [];
  for (let i = 0; i < tokens.length; i += chunkSize) {
    chunks.push(tokens.slice(i, i + chunkSize).join(' '));
  }
  return chunks;
}
```

